[{:seon.task/name "durability-is-continuous-group-commit"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists (merged): continuous group commit, with no window and no clock (DECISIONS: Durability is batched everywhere). `seon.writer/writer-proc` answers a batch once committed in memory and, when the store is dirty and no notice is outstanding, sends the flusher {:op :dirty :commits n}; `seon.writer/flusher-proc` syncs at once (`seon.db/sync!`), one sync covering every notice queued meanwhile, and answers {:op :flushed :commits n}; the writer is clean when n is still its commit count, else it notifies again. A :flush? request (one an outside effect follows) syncs before its answer. `seon.agent/transact!` commits at idle, at turn end and before any wait (`seon.agent/writes-commit-before-any-wait`), never because time passed. Replaced: the flusher's 10 ms sleep on ``seon.db/flush-ms`` and the 10 ms commit clock in `seon.agent/transact!` (both deleted). Not taken: syncing inline after each batch, because a Flow step's answers leave only after its transform returns, so every answer would wait for the fsync. v1 had no flusher: every recording was one synchronous transaction, durable before its reply (~/src/seon CLAUDE.md, \"Recover facts, not execution\"). Cost: per batch one notice; per sync one env sync and one answer; an idle system takes no step."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/the-flush-is-continuous-group-commit}}]
